# ComposeServer — micro-kernel + plugin contract; PLACEMENT UNRATIFIED [SPEC:CXC-COMPOSESERVER]

> ComposeServer is a micro-kernel running on the ComposeBus kernel ([SPEC:CXC-COMPOSEBUS-KERNEL]): it supervises, loads, persists config for, and bus-provisions plugins — nothing else. Any capability that CAN be a plugin MUST be one, including the WS endpoint and the admin UI; the residue left after that test defines the legitimate center. Every plugin is one typed pydantic declaration that DERIVES its own form, announce and registry entry — the author never hand-writes any of the three. A plugin raises its own isolation floor and states a `requires:` execution floor of capabilities and a readiness predicate; unmet, it is PARKED loudly rather than silently degraded. The platform's grammar is closed and platform-owned; a plugin extends the ONTOLOGY through bus namespaces, never the grammar itself.

---

## 1. The micro-kernel boundary

### [CXC-CS-PLACEMENT] Placement is UNRATIFIED, and filename 85 was verified unused

- placement-status: UNRATIFIED. Written into `corpora/platform-specs/` because its nearest relatives (61, 62, 73, 79, 80, 81, 83, 84) already sit there, several of them flagged `[PLACEMENT UNRATIFIED]` themselves — convention, not a ruling.
- filename-number-check: 85 was verified unused across `corpora/platform-specs/` before this file was written (highest prior number in sequence was 83, with 84 authored in this same pass; 99 is a reserved conventions-document number).
- provenance: architect-synthesis
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-COMPOSESERVER]
- see-also: [CXC-VISION-PLACEMENT]

### [CXC-CS-MICROKERNEL] ComposeServer = micro-kernel + plugins; the residue defines the legitimate center

- claim: ComposeServer's kernel does exactly four things — supervise, load, config-persist, bus-provision — and nothing else. Any capability that CAN be a plugin MUST be one; what remains after every plugin-able capability is pulled out IS the legitimate center, not a design choice made independently of that test.
- no-privileged-builtins: there are no privileged built-in services. The WS endpoint and the admin UI are themselves plugins, held to the exact same contract ([CXC-CS-PLUGIN-CONTRACT]) as any third-party capability.
- kernel-publishes-its-own-state: the kernel publishes its own state as `_server/*` — it is a peer on its own bus, not an out-of-band authority, the same discipline [CXC-VISION-ANNOUNCE-BIND]'s level-1 device-to-bus announcement already applies to a leaf node.
- supervisor-not-mediator: the kernel supervises plugin LIFECYCLE (start, stop, park, restart); it never sits on the data path between two plugins. A plugin publishes and subscribes on the bus directly. This is the line [CXC-CBK-CONNECTOR-NEVER-TRANSFORMS] recurses from.
- status: proposed
- provenance: architect-synthesis
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-COMPOSESERVER]
- see-also: [CXC-CBK-CONNECTOR-NEVER-TRANSFORMS], [CXC-VISION-ANNOUNCE-BIND]
- hardening-note: architect-synthesis — David said "there's value in implementing as much of ComposeServer itself as plugins" (line 23298), an aspiration; the node HARDENS that into an imperative ("any capability that CAN be a plugin MUST be one"). The value→MUST hardening is the architect's, not David's stated word.

---

## 2. The plugin contract

### [CXC-CS-PLUGIN-CONTRACT] One typed pydantic declaration derives form, announce and registry — the author writes none of the three by hand

- claim: a plugin is declared as ONE typed pydantic model (`Config` plus `publishes`/`subscribes`). From that single declaration, ComposeServer DERIVES the plugin's config form, its announce payload, and its registry entry.
- announce-is-mandatory-and-derived: every plugin announces; the author never writes the announce logic — it is generated from the same typed declaration that produces the form, so the two can never drift out of sync with each other.
- status: proposed
- provenance: architect-synthesis
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-COMPOSESERVER]
- see-also: [CXC-CS-DYNAMIC-SURFACE], [CXC-CS-ISOLATION-FLOOR]

### [CXC-CS-DYNAMIC-SURFACE] The plugin is authoritative over its own surface and re-announces on change; ComposeServer never caches it as static

- claim: a plugin's published/subscribed surface can change at runtime, and the plugin is the sole authority over its own current surface. On any change it RE-ANNOUNCES — the same on-change discipline [CXC-VISION-ANNOUNCE-BIND] already requires of periodic announcements generally, applied specifically to surface changes. ComposeServer never treats a plugin's surface as fixed at load time; it always defers to the latest announcement.
- change: the `reload:` field is KILLED — removed from the plugin contract as duplicative of re-announce. A plugin whose surface changed already re-announces; a separate `reload:` mechanism would be a second, redundant path to the same fact.
- context_for_change:
  prior_state: an earlier draft of the plugin contract carried a `reload:` field alongside the announce mechanism, as a way for a plugin to signal that ComposeServer should refresh its view of the plugin.
  reasoning: re-announce-on-change already carries that exact signal — a `reload:` field duplicates it rather than adding capability.
  rejected_alternative: keep `reload:` as an explicit, separate refresh signal distinct from a surface-change announcement.
  invalidating_assumption: if a future case needs ComposeServer to refresh something OTHER than the announced surface — a case re-announce cannot express — `reload:` (or its equivalent) would need to return, scoped to that specific gap.
  implication: the plugin loader implements exactly one refresh path (announce-driven), not two.
- status: ratified
- provenance: architect-proposed-david-blessed
- provenance-note: per the dispatch instructions for this authoring pass, killing `reload:` was the architect's call, which David blessed — recorded distinctly from a claim David originated himself.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-COMPOSESERVER]
- see-also: [CXC-CS-PLUGIN-CONTRACT], [CXC-VISION-ANNOUNCE-BIND]

### [CXC-CS-ISOLATION-FLOOR] A plugin raises its own isolation FLOOR; it never forces in-process execution

- claim: a plugin author MAY raise the isolation floor for their own plugin, and a plugin MUST be able to PIN its floor to `subprocess` — a HARD requirement (David's ruling 2026-08-23): if a plugin declares it must run out-of-process, ComposeServer MUST honor that. A plugin may NEVER force `inprocess`, because what can run in-process is dependent on deploy-machine characteristics and other factors unique to the deployment (David, line 23247) — forcing inprocess would lower a floor other plugins rely on ComposeServer to set. ComposeServer sets the actual isolation level to be AT LEAST the declared floor (level >= floor), and may choose stronger isolation than the floor for its own operational reasons.
- status: proposed
- provenance: david-ruling — BOTH halves are David's: no-force-inprocess (line 23247, with his deploy-machine-dependency reason) AND a plugin MUST be able to pin its floor to subprocess (David's Q1 ruling 2026-08-23, a HARD requirement, not merely permitted). The level >= floor formalization is architect synthesis (see level-formalization-note).
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-COMPOSESERVER]
- see-also: [CXC-CS-PLUGIN-CONTRACT], [CXC-CS-REQUIRES-FLOOR]
- level-formalization-note: architect-synthesis — the "actual isolation level >= declared floor" formalization is the architect's, not David's words. David ruled the two directional facts (no-force-inprocess; subprocess-pin MUST be honorable); the ordering formalism expresses them.

---

## 3. The requires: execution floor

### [CXC-CS-REQUIRES-FLOOR] Unmet requires: parks the plugin loudly; ComposeServer is the binder re-evaluating the floor continuously

- claim: `requires:` names an execution floor as a set of CAPABILITIES — announced strings drawn from a registered vocabulary, unversioned in v1 — plus a `ready:` DSL readiness predicate. When the floor is unmet, the plugin is PARKED, and the admin UX names the specific unmet item rather than reporting a generic failure.
- dynamic-rebinding: the floor is re-evaluated continuously, not once at load time — ComposeServer acts as the BINDER standing on the floor, so a plugin parked for a missing capability unparks itself the moment that capability becomes available, with no restart.
- soft-vs-hard: a rule that merely REFERENCES a namespace degrades FALSY when that namespace is absent (soft) — the widget still runs, just evaluates the missing input as false. A `requires:` capability that is absent parks the plugin LOUDLY (hard) — the plugin does not run degraded. The author chooses which failure mode fits their plugin; the two are not the same mechanism wearing different names.
- ready-fails-closed: the `ready:` predicate FAILS CLOSED — when no `ready_evaluator` is supplied, a declared `ready:` predicate is reported UNMET (the plugin parks) rather than assumed satisfied. A park must never be mistaken for readiness, so the ABSENCE of an evaluator parks loudly rather than silently passing. The DSL grammar itself ([SPEC:CXC-DSL]) is out of scope here; the Phase-0 interface carries `ready:` as an opaque expression the binder evaluates.
- ready-fails-closed-provenance: architect-synthesis — realizes [DEC-PHASE0-FLOOR-SEMANTICS-FROZEN]'s `ready-decision`, formalizing this node's own park-loud grain.
- status: proposed
- provenance: david-ruling — David originated the requires: floor, the park-loud-not-silent rationale, the admin-UX-names-the-unmet-item behavior, and the ready: DSL predicate (line 23288). Only the soft-vs-hard degrade contrast is architect synthesis (see soft-vs-hard).
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-COMPOSESERVER]
- see-also: [CXC-CS-ISOLATION-FLOOR], [CXC-CS-ONTOLOGY]

---

## 4. The DSL/ontology boundary

### [CXC-CS-ONTOLOGY] Grammar is closed and platform-owned; a plugin extends the ONTOLOGY via bus namespaces, never the grammar

- claim: the platform's DSL grammar is CLOSED and owned by the platform, never extended per-plugin. A plugin extends what the platform can TALK ABOUT by publishing new bus namespaces — the ontology — not by adding new grammar functions.
- why: a plugin-defined grammar function cannot cross tiers — it cannot run on the CH32 tier's evaluator — so a plugin function would fork the narrow waist the DSL is supposed to be. Extending the ontology instead keeps every tier's evaluator identical; only the set of namespaces it can reference grows.
- realizes: [DX-NARROW-WAIST] — formats/extensions lower to the shared contract (the closed grammar plus the open namespace set) rather than forking a new grammar path per plugin.
- status: proposed
- provenance: architect-synthesis
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-COMPOSESERVER]
- see-also: [CXC-CS-RULE-CONFIG-UX], [DX-NARROW-WAIST]

### [CXC-CS-RULE-CONFIG-UX] Rule config UX is TWO modes (templates + raw DSL), both compiling to ONE validated DSL — the guided middle tier is dropped

- claim: end-user rule authoring is TWO modes, not three (David's Q2 ruling 2026-08-23): (1) templates — a handful of basic canned choices covering the most common use cases; (2) raw DSL — the user hand-types a YAML expression that is validated on submission. Both modes compile down to the SAME validated DSL expression. There is NO "guided structured builder" middle tier: David ruled that complex logic is FORKED OUT of the graphical UX (line 23269), and a graphical logic-builder in the middle is exactly what that excludes.
- validation-is-the-real-evaluator: validation at config time runs the REAL evaluator (the config-time P5/P6b closure), not a separate syntax-only check — so a rule that validates is a rule that will actually evaluate the same way at runtime.
- status: proposed
- provenance: david-ruling — David named the two poles (basic canned choices + hand-typed validated YAML) and ruled complex logic is forked OUT of the graphical UX (line 23269); his Q2 ruling 2026-08-23 confirmed TWO modes, not three, dropping the guided middle tier entirely.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-COMPOSESERVER]
- see-also: [CXC-CS-ONTOLOGY]
- dropped-tier: the earlier draft recorded THREE tiers (templates / guided / raw), where guided = a "structured builder". David's Q2 ruling 2026-08-23 dropped the guided middle tier entirely: a graphical logic-builder is exactly the forked-into-graphical-UX complexity he ruled OUT (line 23269). The surviving two modes both compile to one validated DSL.

### [CXC-CS-DEFAULT-PLUGINS] time and freshness/last-seen ship as DEFAULT plugins, not kernel built-ins

- claim: `time` ships as a DEFAULT plugin — installed by default, but still a plugin, still held to [CXC-CS-PLUGIN-CONTRACT], not special-cased into the kernel (David, line 23279: "make the time plugin part of the standard ComposeServer build"). `freshness`/last-seen is ARCHITECT-PROPOSED and PENDING — it has no origin in David's words in the settling bracket and is NOT yet agreed as a default; it is carried as a proposal, not settled policy (see freshness-status). Either way the pattern is [CXC-CS-MICROKERNEL]'s "any capability that CAN be a plugin MUST be one" applied to capabilities a deployment commonly needs.
- status: proposed
- provenance: architect-synthesis
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-COMPOSESERVER]
- see-also: [CXC-CS-MICROKERNEL]
- freshness-status: architect-proposed / pending — freshness/last-seen has NO origin in David's words in the settling bracket; only `time` is David-sourced (line 23279). It awaits David's agreement before it becomes settled default-plugin policy.

