## 6. Phase 0 — locked plugin mechanics

> This section folds in the 2026-08-23 bus-standup + multi-tenancy design session ([DEC-COMPOSESERVER-BUS-STANDUP-2026-08-23]) and its Phase-0 autonomous build decisions. It makes this spec the correct oracle for the Phase-0 gap-analysis: what the plugin contract now concretely enforces, not only what it was proposed to enforce.

### [CXC-CS-ISOLATION-ENCODING] Isolation is an ordered IntEnum with NO ceiling field — force-inprocess is unrepresentable BY CONSTRUCTION

- claim: isolation is an ORDERED `IntEnum` (`INPROCESS=0 < SUBPROCESS=1`). A plugin declares only a FLOOR; the server sets `actual = max(floor, server_minimum)`. There is deliberately NO ceiling field, so [CXC-CS-ISOLATION-FLOOR]'s rule — a plugin may NEVER force inprocess — is enforced BY CONSTRUCTION, not by a runtime check.
- isolation-prefer: `isolation_prefer` is a separate NON-BINDING hint, distinct from the floor.
- freezes: this freezes [CXC-CS-ISOLATION-FLOOR]'s `level >= floor` formalization into the concrete interface Phase 1 imports.
- why: a prohibition you cannot express is stronger than one you must remember to check — [CXC-CS-ISOLATION-FLOOR]'s park-loud grain, applied to the TYPE rather than to a guard.
- status: proposed — awaiting ratify (Phase-0 autonomous build decision; see [DEC-PHASE0-FLOOR-SEMANTICS-FROZEN] for review)
- provenance: architect-synthesis — realizes [DEC-PHASE0-FLOOR-SEMANTICS-FROZEN]'s `isolation-decision`, itself formalizing David's Q1 HARD-pin ruling already recorded on [CXC-CS-ISOLATION-FLOOR].
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-COMPOSESERVER]
- see-also: [CXC-CS-ISOLATION-FLOOR]

### [CXC-CS-EXTRACTION-INVENTORY] The core-to-plugin extraction inventory — what bus.py currently bakes in that MUST become a plugin, and what legitimately stays kernel

- must-extract: from code inspection of `bus.py`: (1) the reactive BINDINGS ENGINE (`bus.py:1016-1222`) -> the `state_manager` plugin — the kernel currently DOES transforms, violating [SPEC:CXC-COMPOSEBUS-KERNEL]'s [CXC-CBK-CONNECTOR-NEVER-TRANSFORMS] and [DX-CONNECTOR-FORWARDS-NEVER-TRANSFORMS]; extraction enforces the rule and makes `state_manager` largely an EXTRACTION, not a greenfield build. (2) the `_bus/time` publisher -> `time` plugin (receipt-clock + `BusClock` stay kernel). (3) the `_bus/heartbeat` publisher -> `liveness` plugin. (4) module discovery (`_bus/discovery`) -> a plugin. (5) log-mode namespaces -> a `log` plugin.
- default-loaded: TWO of five actually SHED in Phase 0 (2026-08-23): (1) the reactive bindings engine -> `state_manager` plugin, binding via the post-publish hook at the exact former `_evaluate_bindings` site; (4) module discovery -> `discovery` plugin, binding via the module hook at the exact former `_bus/discovery`-send site. Both are default-loaded via `install_default(bus)` at bare-bus construction — see [CXC-CS-DEFAULT-PLUGIN-HOOKS]. THREE remain baked directly in `bus.py`, PENDING Phase 0b: (2) `_bus/time` (the 1 Hz publisher), (3) `_bus/heartbeat` + the liveness-oracle, (5) `publish_log`/log-mode namespaces. These three are PERIODIC/lifecycle-bound rather than event-driven, so extracting them requires the bus start/stop lifecycle-hook seam the event-driven hooks cannot carry — see [CXC-CS-LIFECYCLE-HOOKS].
- legitimately-kernel: freshness verdict-PUBLISHING does not exist yet (`freshness_report` returns a plain dict) — nothing to extract there. The receipt-timestamp and the read-path verdict are legitimately kernel. The namespace registrar (`ns_announce.py`, COMPACT-WIRE) is already OUTSIDE `bus.py`, already on the registry-plugin trajectory. `countdown`'s `_countdown_claims` dict leaked into the kernel constructor and moves with `countdown` when IT becomes a plugin.
- status: proposed — awaiting ratify (Phase-0 autonomous build decision; see [DEC-COMPOSESERVER-BUS-STANDUP-2026-08-23]'s `extractions-determined`/`surprises-resolved` for review; corrected 2026-08-23 against the landed code and [DEC-PHASE0B-LIFECYCLE-HOOK-SEAM-AND-DEFAULT-SHEDS])
- provenance: architect-synthesis — realizes the `extractions-determined` and `surprises-resolved` properties of [DEC-COMPOSESERVER-BUS-STANDUP-2026-08-23]; the `default-loaded` property was corrected against the landed Phase-0 code (`origin/main` `ec83f69`) and [DEC-PHASE0B-LIFECYCLE-HOOK-SEAM-AND-DEFAULT-SHEDS], per the dispatch instruction that extraction mechanics carry the architect-synthesis tag.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-COMPOSESERVER]
- see-also: [CXC-CS-MICROKERNEL], [CXC-CS-DEFAULT-PLUGIN-HOOKS], [CXC-CS-LIFECYCLE-HOOKS], [SPEC:CXC-COMPOSEBUS-KERNEL]

### [CXC-CS-DEFAULT-PLUGIN-HOOKS] Default plugins install at bare-bus construction via generic kernel HOOK seams the kernel fires but does not own

- claim: the shed capabilities ([CXC-CS-EXTRACTION-INVENTORY]) bind to the kernel through GENERIC hook seams: `register_post_publish_hook(ns, new)` fired at the exact former `_evaluate_bindings` site; `register_module_hook(module)` fired at the exact former `_bus/discovery`-send site; `set_liveness_oracle(fn)` consulted by `node_is_alive` (see [SPEC:CXC-COMPOSEBUS-KERNEL]'s [CXC-CBK-LIVENESS-ORACLE-HOOK]). A bare `ComposeIOBus()` default-installs the shed engines (`state_manager`, `discovery`) at construction via lazy `install_default(bus)` functions living in `composeio/plugins/`.
- kernel-stays-ignorant: the hook seams keep the kernel free of any plugin's identity — it fires hooks, never imports a plugin by name on the hot path. `install_default` is the standard-build default-load [CXC-CS-DEFAULT-PLUGINS] mandates.
- ordering-preserved: each hook fires at the IDENTICAL call site the baked-in code used, so ordering is preserved and hundreds of tests that construct bare buses keep passing unmodified.
- status: proposed — awaiting ratify (Phase-0 autonomous build decision; see [DEC-PHASE0-DEFAULT-PLUGINS-VIA-KERNEL-HOOKS] for review)
- provenance: architect-synthesis — realizes [DEC-PHASE0-DEFAULT-PLUGINS-VIA-KERNEL-HOOKS].
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-COMPOSESERVER]
- see-also: [CXC-CS-DEFAULT-PLUGINS], [CXC-CS-EXTRACTION-INVENTORY], [SPEC:CXC-COMPOSEBUS-KERNEL]

### [CXC-CS-LIFECYCLE-HOOKS] A bus start/stop lifecycle-hook seam — register_start_hook/register_stop_hook — carries PERIODIC default plugins the event-driven hooks cannot

- claim: `register_start_hook(hook(bus))` / `register_stop_hook(hook(bus))` are a GENERIC kernel hook seam, SYMMETRIC to [CXC-CS-DEFAULT-PLUGIN-HOOKS]'s `register_post_publish_hook`/`register_module_hook`: the kernel FIRES them at the exact sites it starts and stops its BusClock ticks, but does not OWN what a plugin attaches there.
- why: the three capability sheds [CXC-CS-EXTRACTION-INVENTORY] lists as PENDING — `_bus/time` (the 1 Hz publisher), `_bus/heartbeat` + the liveness-oracle, and `publish_log` — are PERIODIC/lifecycle-bound, unlike the event-driven post-publish and module hooks already landed; a plugin attaches its periodic publisher at start and detaches it at stop with no orphan tick.
- pattern-mirrored: the three sheds mirror the Phase-0 `state_manager`/`discovery` pattern exactly — a bare `ComposeIOBus()` installs a default engine so behavior is byte-identical before/after, and `TimePlugin`/`LivenessPlugin`/`LogPlugin` (all `default_loaded`) wear the same engine for the standup/registry path.
- publish-log-stays-a-method: `publish_log` STAYS a callable bus method delegating to the default `LogPlugin` engine — behavior-preservation over a purer "publish to the log namespace directly" shape.
- landed-status: LANDED 2026-08-24 on origin/main at 46dda5d (Phase 0b). register_start_hook/register_stop_hook exist in bus.py, symmetric to the event-driven hooks; TimePlugin/LivenessPlugin/LogPlugin ship default_loaded; diamond-verified (first-bringup PASS, gap-analyzer CONFORM after the P1 announce-keying fix).
- status: proposed — awaiting ratify (Phase-0b autonomous build decision; see [DEC-PHASE0B-LIFECYCLE-HOOK-SEAM-AND-DEFAULT-SHEDS] for review)
- provenance: architect-autonomous — decided under David's overnight autonomy grant; realizes [DEC-PHASE0B-LIFECYCLE-HOOK-SEAM-AND-DEFAULT-SHEDS].
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-COMPOSESERVER]
- see-also: [CXC-CS-DEFAULT-PLUGIN-HOOKS], [CXC-CS-EXTRACTION-INVENTORY], [SPEC:CXC-COMPOSEBUS-KERNEL]

### [CXC-CS-LIVENESS-FRESHNESS-SPLIT] The four ambiguous rulings — node-liveness and freshness-classes are PLUGIN, dynamic selectors are KERNEL, critical/ACK moves to peer/critical.py

- ruling: David ratified the architect's leans on four ambiguous calls, 2026-08-23: (1) node-liveness = PLUGIN (publisher + receiver), with the kernel consulting it via a sanctioned liveness-oracle hook — see [SPEC:CXC-COMPOSEBUS-KERNEL]'s [CXC-CBK-LIVENESS-ORACLE-HOOK]. (2) freshness classes (multi-namespace aggregation) = PLUGIN; a single-namespace freshness VERDICT stays kernel — the two are different grains. (3) dynamic selectors = KERNEL (addressing, like scope — not a derived fact). (4) critical/ACK helpers move to `peer/critical.py` (transport QoS, out of bus-core) — see [SPEC:CXC-COMPOSEBUS-KERNEL]'s [CXC-CBK-SEND-MESSAGE-SEAM] for the kernel-side seam this leaves behind.
- status: decided
- provenance: david-ruling — David ratified all four leans on [DEC-COMPOSESERVER-BUS-STANDUP-2026-08-23]'s `ambiguous-rulings` property.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-COMPOSESERVER]
- see-also: [CXC-CS-MICROKERNEL], [SPEC:CXC-COMPOSEBUS-KERNEL]

### [CXC-CS-LIVENESS-FALLBACK-SELF-EXCLUDED] Default LivenessPlugin stays FALLBACK not oracle; a node's own heartbeat is EXCLUDED from its own liveness verdict

- ruling: David ratified two liveness-semantics rulings 2026-08-24, resolving open questions [CXC-CS-LIVENESS-FRESHNESS-SPLIT] left unsettled and that the Phase-0b diamond surfaced. **D1** — the default `LivenessPlugin` REMAINS THE FALLBACK, never self-installs as the liveness oracle: the `set_liveness_oracle` seat stays EMPTY for the default plugin; the kernel falls back to the plugin's own heartbeat-receiver when the seat is empty; a richer liveness plugin later TAKES the seat and supersedes. **D2** — a node's OWN heartbeat is EXCLUDED from its OWN `node_is_alive` verdict: `node_is_alive(self.bus_id)` is answered by "am I running," not by receipt of a beat the node itself emitted; the heartbeat receiver filters `source == own bus_id`.
- d1-rationale: preserves the clean "empty seat → built-in receiver" supersession path and the liveness/freshness split ([CXC-CS-LIVENESS-FRESHNESS-SPLIT]); self-installing would force a later richer plugin to contend for the single seat.
- d2-rationale: a node counting its own beat toward its own liveness verdict is circular, and a self-beat can mask a dead same-bus_id peer or inflate fleet-liveness counts.
- status: decided
- provenance: david-ruling — ratified 2026-08-24 via `docs/composeserver/DECISIONS-phase1-plan.md` (D1, D2).
- dsd-source: docs/composeserver/DECISIONS-phase1-plan.md (D1, D2)
- self-alive-definition: self-alive = bus RUNNING (`is_clock_running`, set by `start()`, cleared by `stop()`) AND liveness engine INSTALLED (`LivenessEngine.is_installed`); False after `bus.stop()` or after the liveness plugin is uninstalled. Refines D2's "am I running" intent to the landed operational check — the first implementation made `_self_is_alive()` sticky-True for the whole object lifetime (true even after stop/uninstall), a gap the diamond found and this fixes. DEFERRED, NOT ratified: whether a stopped-then-restarted bus's self-liveness returns to True (resumable-pause vs terminal `bus.stop()` semantics) — the landed code re-reads True on re-`start()` as a natural resumable-pause consequence, but the terminal-vs-resumable policy itself is unresolved and flagged for David. See [DEC-PHASE1-LIVENESS-SELF-ALIVE-TRACKS-RUNNING-STATE] (provenance architect-autonomous, faithful implementation of D2's intent, not a new policy).
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-COMPOSESERVER]
- see-also: [CXC-CS-LIVENESS-FRESHNESS-SPLIT], [CXC-CBK-LIVENESS-ORACLE-HOOK], [SPEC:CXC-COMPOSEBUS-KERNEL]

